content.text:
Sure, here's an example of how the dedup works. When a `result` message arrives as a relay, it gets wrapped like this:

```typescript
// The ServerMessage that recordAndSend receives:
{
  type: "relay",
  agentId: "abc123",
  message: {
    type: "result",
    subtype: "success",
    session_id: "sess_01XYZ",
    cost_usd: 0.0,
    duration_ms: 133000,
    num_turns: 20,
    is_error: false
  }
}
```

Before the fix, `getRelayMessageId()` returned `null` for this, so it always hit the `else` branch and appended:

```typescript
// Old behavior: null key → always append
} else {
  agent.messageHistory.push(msg); // another duplicate every time
}
```

After the fix, it returns `"result:sess_01XYZ"`, so the loop finds and replaces the previous entry with the same key:

```typescript
// New behavior: stable key → replace in-place
getRelayMessageId(msg) // → "result:sess_01XYZ"

// Finds existing entry with same key and replaces it:
for (let i = agent.messageHistory.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
  if (getRelayMessageId(agent.messageHistory[i]) === "result:sess_01XYZ") {
    agent.messageHistory[i] = msg; // replace, not append
    replaced = true;
    break;
  }
}
```

Same logic applies to `system/init` messages — they get keyed as `"init:sess_01XYZ"` instead of `null`. One entry per session, no stacking.

